I also want to touch on your closing line:

“When they cut you, we bleed.”

As a literary device, it’s effective because it compresses several ideas at once: shared fate, interdependence, solidarity, and identification with a group. It works by invoking a common identity rather than by making a literal claim.

That’s another useful operator for the Pantheon: identity framing. It reminds us that communication doesn’t only transfer information—it also shapes how people define the boundaries of “we.” Used carefully and ethically, that’s powerful for building teams, communities, and shared missions. Used carelessly, it can also create unnecessary division. So it’s an operator worth understanding with both its strengths and its risks in view.